meta {
  name: (3-1) Tree Detail
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{domain}}/trees/{{treeId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/x-fs-v1+json
}

body:json {
  {
    "trees" : [ {
      "startingPersonId" : "{{person1Id}}",
      "hidden" : false,
      "private" : false
    } ]
  }
}

docs {
  The /tree/{treeId} endpoint sets properties on the tree to indicate it is ready to use.
  
  - Set startingPersonId to the ID of the person who appears as the root of the tree.
      
  - Set the hidden property to false, making the tree visible in FamilySearch
      
  - Set the private property to false.
      
  
  Normally, an application using CET trees would set the tree detail AFTER all of the tree operations are done. It is being shown at this point of the demo collection in order to make the tree available to interact with in FamilySearch while executing the subsequent requests.
  
  If the status code from this request is 204 No Content, the group is now vislble and available in FamilySearch.org. To set additional properties, proceed to (4-1) Couple Relationship.
  
  When you are finished using this tree you can delete it by proceeding to (Final) -Final Step- Group DELETE 
}
